Code Review Architecture — Pulse Engine
Overview

4 CMake targets (Engine static lib, GameModule shared lib, PulseEditor, PulseGame), all via file(GLOB_RECURSE ...) — convenient but fragile for incremental builds/CI. No split between include/ (public) and src/ (internal): any file in the repo can include any engine header. Worse, the engine is aware of its consumers through macros (BUILD_ENGINE/BUILD_GAME/BUILD_EDITOR) that change the behavior of the headers themselves — the expected dependency inversion (neutral engine, consumers adapt) is broken.

Most Structural Issue: Global Service Locator

Core::EngineInstance (src/engine/core/engine.hpp) is a Meyer's singleton exposing all subsystems (Renderer, ECS managers, Physics, Audio, Time, Project...) via GetXManager(), plus a duplicated global pointer gSharedEnginePtr. Editor, game, and engine all access it directly (Core::GetEngine()...) rather than through injection. This is the main driver of global coupling: everything is mutable from anywhere, which will hurt testability, headless engine reuse, and reasoning about subsystem lifecycle.

Homegrown ECS Rather Than Data-Oriented

No entt: a classic Actor/Component hierarchy (shared_ptr, virtual dispatch, Unity GameObject style) with a hand-built reflection system (.reflection.hpp files per component). Internally consistent, but not a cache-friendly ECS, and the base Component directly includes JSON + Actor in its header — serialization is baked into the base class instead of being externalized.

Renderer: Partial God-Object

renderer.cpp (~700 lines) centralizes pass scheduling, pipeline caching, draw-command batching, and framebuffer management in a single class, even though the subfolders (pipeline, framebuffer, material, mesh, lighting) show the beginnings of proper decomposition. shadow_manager.cpp (~614 lines) is heavily coupled to the renderer's internals. An explicit TODO in the header (RendererSettings should live in a "setting manager") confirms this is a known gap, not just an outside observation.

Porous Editor/Engine Boundary

The link direction is correct (Editor → Engine), but the editor UI (properties_panel.cpp, 830 lines — the largest file in the repo — viewport_window.cpp, main_window.cpp) calls Core::GetEngine() directly and manipulates concrete/reflected component types instead of going through a dedicated editor API. Result: the editor isn't really a layer on top of the engine — it's entangled with its internals.

Serialization Inconsistent with the Reflection System

The engine has a per-component reflection system (.reflection.hpp), but level.cpp deserializes actors through an explicit chain of downcasts per concrete type (Transform, model, light, PhysicsBody, camera, audio) instead of going through a generic factory driven by the existing reflection system. Two mechanisms that should converge but don't.

Coupling: Event System → Physics

event_system.hpp (otherwise generic and well-designed: 4 subscription scopes, clean type erasure) directly includes physics_manager.hpp for physics-specific events — an event bus meant to be generic depends on a concrete subsystem.

Total Absence of Tests

No framework (GTest/Catch2), no enable_testing()/add_test in the CMakeLists. test_project/ is a sample project, not a test suite. Zero automated coverage on an engine of this size — this is the highest medium-term risk (silent regressions like the one you just fixed in rendering).

Priorities If You Want to Act
Tests — even a minimal foundation (GTest + CI) on ECS/serialization/events would have the best effort-to-risk-reduction ratio.
Decouple EngineInstance — at minimum separate "read access to subsystems" from "mutation," or introduce explicit injection for new subsystems rather than extending the service locator.
Converge reflection and level serialization (level.cpp vs .reflection.hpp) to avoid double maintenance.
Split the Renderer by separating pass scheduling / pipeline caching / draw-command submission.